The CIA Triad: There are 3 Pillars that make up the basis of security and secure programming practices.

Confidentiality - Defined by access controls for private data, and practices prevent unauthorized access to sections of data. 
Common tools for this are Encryption, and multi-factor-authorization

Integrity - The process by which a system is able to guarentee that data is accurate, complete, and trustworthy. 
The most common tools for this are digital signatures, and version control.

Availability - The systems process that gives users "uptime" where systems are accesssible and prevents "downtime" where systems are inaccessible.
The most common tools for this process are backups and redundancy.


App Logic:

The main core of the logic is centered on defining role based accessibility through permissions, 
and each role provides the systems by which the program displays the necessary information.

For the moment in order to test each role, the user specific role planned to be loggin into needs to be hardcoded.
